1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,411
This is ECE 320, video 14 b. It continues video 14 a with set-associativity and replacement policies.

2
00:00:10,861 --> 00:00:24,898
After this video, you can find the bits for every cache organization, and simulate a set-associative cache with LRU. Then we solve assignment 5 and the cache questions of two past midterms.

3
00:00:25,348 --> 00:00:44,399
Two blocks with the same index conflict. Set-associativity fixes this: a block can go in one of a few frames. The frames form groups called sets, and each frame in a set is a way. Here, 8 frames form 4 sets of 2 ways: a 2-way set-associative cache.

4
00:00:44,849 --> 00:00:50,673
A cache with 1 way per set is direct-mapped. That is the cache of video 14 a.

5
00:00:51,123 --> 00:01:02,408
More ways give fewer sets, so fewer index bits. A cache with only 1 set is fully associative: a block can go in any frame, and there are no index bits.

6
00:01:02,858 --> 00:01:12,010
The plus: fewer conflicts. The minus: a longer t hit, because the cache must compare more tags and select the data with a mux.

7
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:33,196
Here is the 32 kilobyte cache with 32-byte blocks, now 2-way. It has 512 sets of 2 ways, so still 1024 frames. Way 0 holds frames 0 to 511, and way 1 holds frames 512 to 1023.

8
00:01:33,646 --> 00:01:56,387
The block has 32 bytes, so the offset is 5 bits. There are 512 sets, so the index is log 2 of 512, which is 9 bits. The tag is the rest, 18 bits. The direct-mapped cache had a 10-bit index and a 17-bit tag. So each doubling of the ways moves one bit from the index to the tag.

9
00:01:56,837 --> 00:02:10,597
The lookup algorithm. The index bits select the set. Address hex C 0 4 4 has index 2 and tag 3. The cache reads the tags and the data of both ways of set 2 in parallel.

10
00:02:11,047 --> 00:02:26,023
Each way has its own comparator. If any way has a tag match and its valid bit set, it is a hit. Here way 1 holds tag 3, so way 1 hits, and a mux selects its data. If no way matches, it is a miss.

11
00:02:26,473 --> 00:02:46,207
Slide 25 repeats the paper simulation with a 2-way cache: 8 bytes, 2-byte blocks. Pause and try it. There are 2 sets. The offset is 1 bit, the index is 1 bit, and the tag is 2 bits. At the start, way 0 is the least recently used way in each set.

12
00:02:46,657 --> 00:02:58,390
Access 1 1 0 0. Tag 1 1, set 0. Set 0 holds tags 0 0 and 0 1. Miss. LRU replaces way 0.

13
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:05,859
Access 1 1 1 0. Tag 1 1, set 1. Miss. Replace way 0 of set 1.

14
00:03:06,309 --> 00:03:15,482
Access 1 0 0 0. Tag 1 0, set 0. Miss. Way 0 was just used, so replace way 1.

15
00:03:15,932 --> 00:03:23,313
Access 0 0 1 1. Tag 0 0, set 1. Miss. Replace way 1 of set 1.

16
00:03:23,763 --> 00:03:30,014
Access 1 0 0 0. Set 0, way 1 holds tag 1 0. Hit.

17
00:03:30,464 --> 00:03:39,659
Access 0 0 0 0. Tag 0 0, set 0. Miss. Way 1 was just used, so replace way 0.

18
00:03:40,109 --> 00:03:48,813
Access 1 0 0 0. It is still in way 1. Hit! In the direct-mapped cache this access was a conflict miss.

19
00:03:49,263 --> 00:04:07,652
Plus: 0 0 0 0 and 1 0 0 0 can now both be in set 0. Minus: new conflicts can appear. 0 0 1 1 hit in the direct-mapped cache, but now it misses. In practice, conflict avoidance usually wins.

20
00:04:08,102 --> 00:04:24,358
Set-associativity adds a design choice. On a miss in a full set, which block is replaced? An example, not from the slides: a 4-way set gets A, B, C and D. A loop then uses A again and again. Then E misses.

21
00:04:24,808 --> 00:04:37,693
Random replacement picks any way. FIFO, first in first out, replaces the block that came in first: A. But A is in use by the loop. So FIFO does not match temporal locality.

22
00:04:38,143 --> 00:04:49,898
LRU, least recently used, replaces B, the block with the oldest use. LRU fits temporal locality: that block is the least likely to be used soon.

23
00:04:50,348 --> 00:05:03,703
NMRU, not most recently used, replaces any block except the most recent one. It is easier to build than LRU. For a 2-way cache, NMRU is the same as LRU.

24
00:05:04,153 --> 00:05:17,337
Belady's policy replaces the block that will be used furthest in the future. Hardware cannot know the future. So it is an unachievable optimum, used only to compare the other policies.

25
00:05:17,787 --> 00:05:38,309
How does the cache find the least recently used block? Each block in a set has an age count. In a 4-way cache, 2 extra bits per block hold an age from 0 to 3. When a block is used or replaced, its age becomes 0. Every age lower than its previous age gets plus 1.

26
00:05:38,759 --> 00:05:43,730
An example. The 4 blocks of a set have ages 3, 0, 2 and 1.

27
00:05:44,180 --> 00:06:01,353
The program uses block 2. Its previous age was 2, so it becomes 0. The ages lower than 2 are block 1 with 0 and block 3 with 1. They become 1 and 2. Block 0 has age 3, which is not lower, so it stays 3.

28
00:06:01,803 --> 00:06:08,694
The block with the largest age is the least recently used. On the next miss, LRU replaces it.

29
00:06:09,144 --> 00:06:19,512
Slide 29 runs the algorithm on one 4-way set. Block 0 is empty. Blocks 1, 2 and 3 have ages 0, 2 and 1.

30
00:06:19,962 --> 00:06:27,621
A hit in block 3. Its age 1 becomes 0. Only block 1 has a lower age, so it becomes 1.

31
00:06:28,071 --> 00:06:40,167
A miss. The data moves into the empty block 0, with age 0. An empty block has no previous age, so all the other ages get plus 1: 2, 3 and 1.

32
00:06:40,617 --> 00:06:52,862
Another miss. The set is full. Block 2 has the largest age, 3, so the data replaces block 2. Its age becomes 0, and all the other ages get plus 1.

33
00:06:53,312 --> 00:07:04,725
A hit in block 3. Its age 2 becomes 0. Blocks 0 and 2 had lower ages, so they get plus 1. Block 1 is now the least recently used.

34
00:07:05,175 --> 00:07:10,338
Higher associativity gives a lower miss rate, because there are fewer conflicts.

35
00:07:10,788 --> 00:07:20,388
But t hit increases, because of the extra compares and the wider mux. The designer must balance both terms of the average access time.

36
00:07:20,838 --> 00:07:35,025
The block size and the number of sets are usually powers of two. Then the offset, index and tag are just groups of address bits. The hardware takes them out of the address with wires, and needs no arithmetic.

37
00:07:35,475 --> 00:07:51,987
Assignment 5, question 1. The block size is 32 bytes, the cache holds 16384 blocks, and addresses are 32 bits. Find the bits for three organizations. Pause the video and try it.

38
00:07:52,437 --> 00:08:00,479
The block size does not change, so the offset is log 2 of 32, which is 5 bits, in all three cases.

39
00:08:00,929 --> 00:08:20,919
Part a, 2-way. 16384 blocks divided by 2 ways is 8192 sets. Log 2 of 8192 is 13 index bits. The tag is 32 minus 5 minus 13, which is 14 bits.

40
00:08:21,369 --> 00:08:31,566
Part b, fully associative. There is only 1 set, so 0 index bits. The tag is 32 minus 5, which is 27 bits.

41
00:08:32,016 --> 00:08:50,725
Part c, direct-mapped. 1 way, so 16384 sets. Log 2 of 16384 is 14 index bits. The tag is 13 bits. Check: from direct-mapped to 2-way, one bit moves from the index to the tag.

42
00:08:51,175 --> 00:09:13,362
Assignment 5, question 2. Three caches, each with 8 blocks of 16 bytes and LRU. The addresses are 12 bits, in hex. The offset is log 2 of 16, 4 bits: exactly the last hex digit. So the first two hex digits are the block address, the tag and index together. The tables hold these.

43
00:09:13,812 --> 00:09:31,007
Direct-mapped: 8 sets, so 3 index bits and 5 tag bits. For hex 1 A 3, the block address is 1 A, binary 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 0. The index is the low 3 bits, 0 1 0, so set 2.

44
00:09:31,457 --> 00:09:38,219
Fully associative: 1 set, so no index bits. The tag is all 8 bits of the block address.

45
00:09:38,669 --> 00:09:52,728
4-way: 2 sets, so 1 index bit and 7 tag bits. The index is the last bit of the block address. 1 A is even, so set 0. Pause the video and fill the three tables yourself first.

46
00:09:53,178 --> 00:10:03,461
Part a, direct-mapped. Address 1 1 0. The block address is 1 1. Its low 3 bits are 0 0 1, so block 1. Miss.

47
00:10:03,911 --> 00:10:09,137
1 3 6. Block address 1 3, index 3. Miss. Fill block 3.

48
00:10:09,587 --> 00:10:15,433
2 0 2. Block address 2 0, index 0. Miss. Fill block 0.

49
00:10:15,883 --> 00:10:19,339
1 A 3. Block address 1 A, index 2. Miss.

50
00:10:19,789 --> 00:10:29,687
1 0 2. Block address 1 0, index 0. Block 0 holds 2 0. Miss. 1 0 replaces 2 0.

51
00:10:30,137 --> 00:10:34,425
3 6 1. Block address 3 6, index 6. Miss.

52
00:10:34,875 --> 00:10:44,347
2 0 4. Block address 2 0, index 0 again. Block 0 holds 1 0. Miss. 2 0 comes back.

53
00:10:44,797 --> 00:10:51,069
1 1 4. Block address 1 1, index 1. Block 1 holds 1 1. Hit.

54
00:10:51,519 --> 00:10:54,933
1 A 4. Block address 1 A, index 2. Hit.

55
00:10:55,383 --> 00:11:00,033
1 7 7. Block address 1 7, index 7. Miss.

56
00:11:00,483 --> 00:11:08,121
3 0 1. Block address 3 0, index 0. Miss. 3 0 replaces 2 0.

57
00:11:08,571 --> 00:11:14,395
2 0 6. Index 0 again. Block 0 holds 3 0. Miss.

58
00:11:14,845 --> 00:11:26,941
1 3 5. Index 3. Hit. Total: 10 misses in 13. Blocks 2 0, 1 0 and 3 0 all need block 0, so they kick each other out.

59
00:11:27,391 --> 00:11:47,977
Part b, fully associative. A block can go in any of the 8 ways, and the tag is the whole block address. The first six accesses are six different blocks: 1 1, 1 3, 2 0, 1 A, 1 0 and 3 6. Each one misses and fills the next empty way, ways 0 to 5.

60
00:11:48,427 --> 00:11:55,638
2 0 4. Block 2 0 is still in way 2. Hit. In the direct-mapped cache, this was a miss.

61
00:11:56,088 --> 00:12:00,547
1 1 4 and 1 A 4 also hit, in ways 0 and 3.

62
00:12:00,997 --> 00:12:05,541
1 7 7. Block 1 7 is new. Miss. Fill way 6.

63
00:12:05,991 --> 00:12:12,860
3 0 1. Block 3 0 is new. Miss. Fill way 7. Now all 8 ways are full.

64
00:12:13,310 --> 00:12:17,214
2 0 6 and 1 3 5 hit, in ways 2 and 1.

65
00:12:17,664 --> 00:12:25,408
Total: 8 misses in 13 accesses. There are exactly 8 different blocks, and each one misses only once.

66
00:12:25,858 --> 00:12:40,685
Part c, 4-way. The index is the last bit of the block address. Odd block addresses go to set 1. 1 1 0 and 1 3 6 have odd block addresses, so they fill ways 0 and 1 of set 1.

67
00:12:41,135 --> 00:12:51,844
2 0 2, 1 A 3, 1 0 2 and 3 6 1 have even block addresses. They fill the 4 ways of set 0. Set 0 is now full.

68
00:12:52,294 --> 00:12:57,350
2 0 4. Block 2 0 is in set 0, way 0. Hit.

69
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:03,837
1 1 4 hits in set 1, way 0. 1 A 4 hits in set 0, way 1.

70
00:13:04,287 --> 00:13:09,301
1 7 7. Odd, so set 1. Miss. Fill the empty way 2.

71
00:13:09,751 --> 00:13:29,079
3 0 1. Even, so set 0. Miss, and set 0 is full. List the uses of set 0. 2 0 and 1 A were used again later, and 3 6 came after 1 0. So 1 0 is the least recently used. 3 0 replaces it in way 2.

72
00:13:29,529 --> 00:13:35,971
2 0 6 hits in set 0, way 0. 1 3 5 hits in set 1, way 1.

73
00:13:36,421 --> 00:13:49,179
The summary. Direct-mapped: 10 misses in 13. Fully associative and 4-way: 8 misses each. Associativity removed the 2 conflict misses of the direct-mapped cache.

74
00:13:49,629 --> 00:14:04,349
Now the Fall 2022 midterm, question 2. Part A, 3 marks. Two 4-way caches with 16-bit addresses. Find the tag, index and offset bits of both. Pause the video and try it.

75
00:14:04,799 --> 00:14:22,441
Cache 1. 1024 divided by 16 is 64 blocks. 64 divided by 4 ways is 16 sets. So the offset is 4 bits, the index is 4 bits, and the tag is 16 minus 8, which is 8 bits.

76
00:14:22,891 --> 00:14:41,046
Cache 2. 16384 divided by 256 is also 64 blocks, so also 16 sets and a 4-bit index. The offset is log 2 of 256, 8 bits. The tag is 4 bits.

77
00:14:41,496 --> 00:14:47,384
Both caches have 16 sets. The larger blocks move bits from the tag to the offset.

78
00:14:47,834 --> 00:14:57,007
Part B, 5 marks. Which cache gives the higher hit rate for this program? First, find the addresses. Pause the video and try it.

79
00:14:57,457 --> 00:15:16,679
Each row of A is 4096 bytes, hex 1 0 0 0, and each element is 4 bytes. So A of j, i is at 4096 times j plus 4 times i. B of i is at hex 4 0 1 0 plus 4 times i.

80
00:15:17,129 --> 00:15:32,105
The inner loop runs over j, so the program walks down a column of A: A 0 0, A 1 0, A 2 0, A 3 0, then B 0. Then the same for i equal to 1. That gives 10 addresses.

81
00:15:32,555 --> 00:15:58,816
Cache 1 has 16-byte blocks: tag bits 15 to 8, index bits 7 to 4, offset bits 3 to 0. The first four addresses, hex 0, 1 0 0 0, 2 0 0 0 and 3 0 0 0, all have index 0. Their tags are 0 0, 1 0, 2 0 and 3 0. Four misses fill the 4 ways of set 0.

82
00:15:59,266 --> 00:16:11,426
B of 0 is at hex 4 0 1 0. Bits 7 to 4 are 0 0 0 1, so the index is 1. The tag is 4 0. Miss, into set 1.

83
00:16:11,876 --> 00:16:22,671
Now i is 1. A 0 1 is at hex 4, in the same 16-byte block as A 0 0. Hit. A 1 1, A 2 1 and A 3 1 hit too.

84
00:16:23,121 --> 00:16:28,881
B of 1 is at hex 4 0 1 4, the same block as B of 0. Hit.

85
00:16:29,331 --> 00:16:34,344
Cache 1 has 5 hits in 10 accesses. The hit rate is 50 percent.

86
00:16:34,794 --> 00:16:57,279
Cache 2 has 256-byte blocks: tag bits 15 to 12, index bits 11 to 8, offset bits 7 to 0. Hex 0, 1 0 0 0, 2 0 0 0 and 3 0 0 0 all have index 0, with tags 0 to 3. Four misses fill set 0.

87
00:16:57,729 --> 00:17:14,305
B of 0 at hex 4 0 1 0. Bits 11 to 8 are 0 0 0 0, so the index is also 0, with tag 4. Set 0 is full. The least recently used block is tag 0. It is evicted.

88
00:17:14,755 --> 00:17:24,291
A 0 1 at hex 4 has tag 0, which was just evicted. Miss. LRU now evicts tag 1, which is the next block needed.

89
00:17:24,741 --> 00:17:32,485
This repeats. Five blocks take turns in a 4-way set, and LRU always evicts the block that comes next.

90
00:17:32,935 --> 00:17:43,538
Cache 2 has 0 hits in 10 accesses. So cache 1, with the 16-byte blocks, has the higher hit rate: 50 percent against 0 percent.

91
00:17:43,988 --> 00:17:57,321
Part C, 2 marks. For cache 2, can we get a higher hit rate without a larger cache? Pause and try it. The problem: 5 blocks map to set 0, but the set has only 4 ways.

92
00:17:57,771 --> 00:18:15,734
Yes. Keep 16384 bytes and 256-byte blocks, but make the cache 8-way: 8 sets with a 3-bit index. The 5 blocks still go to set 0, but now they fit. 5 misses, then 5 hits: 50 percent.

93
00:18:16,184 --> 00:18:24,419
Smaller blocks also work. With 16-byte blocks, A goes to set 0 and B to set 1: also 50 percent.

94
00:18:24,869 --> 00:18:31,909
So the answer is yes. These are conflict misses: raise the associativity or use smaller blocks.

95
00:18:32,359 --> 00:18:50,684
The last exam question: Fall 2019, question 3. A 32 kilobyte, 4-way cache with 16-byte blocks. Part A asks for the bits. Part B asks for 5 addresses in the same set but in different ways. Pause the video and try it.

96
00:18:51,134 --> 00:19:14,857
Part A. The offset is log 2 of 16, 4 bits. 32 kilobytes divided by 16 bytes is 2048 blocks. Divided by 4 ways, that is 512 sets, so 9 index bits. The paper does not give the address width. Assume 32 bits, as in RISC-V, and say so. Then the tag is 19 bits.

97
00:19:15,307 --> 00:19:41,760
Part B. Same set means the same index bits. Different ways means different tags. So keep the index and offset at 0, and change only the tag. One tag step is 512 times 16 bytes, hex 2 0 0 0. So A to E are hex 0, 2 0 0 0, 4 0 0 0, 6 0 0 0 and 8 0 0 0.

98
00:19:42,210 --> 00:20:01,218
Part C. The table shows 15 accesses to one set. They go to ways 0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 1, and so on, and every access misses. Fill in the access labels. Pause and try it. The first four accesses fill the empty ways: A, B, C, D.

99
00:20:01,668 --> 00:20:12,655
Access 5 must miss and replace way 0. So it is the new block, E. LRU replaces A, the least recently used block, in way 0.

100
00:20:13,105 --> 00:20:22,811
Access 6 must miss and use way 1. A was just evicted, so access A again. It misses, and LRU replaces B in way 1.

101
00:20:23,261 --> 00:20:31,816
The pattern continues: B, C, D, E, A, B, C, D, E. Each access asks for a block that is not in the set.

102
00:20:32,266 --> 00:20:41,311
The rule: 5 blocks cycling through a 4-way LRU set miss every time, as in cache 2 of Fall 2022.

103
00:20:41,761 --> 00:20:50,039
The recap. One. Sets equal blocks divided by ways. Direct-mapped has 1 way. Fully associative has 1 set.

104
00:20:50,489 --> 00:20:57,891
Two. The offset is log 2 of the block size, the index is log 2 of the sets, and the tag is the rest.

105
00:20:58,341 --> 00:21:03,909
Three. The index selects the set, and all tags of the set are compared in parallel.

106
00:21:04,359 --> 00:21:08,946
Four. Associativity removes conflicts but makes t hit longer.

107
00:21:09,396 --> 00:21:18,719
Five. LRU ages: the used block gets age 0, and the lower ages get plus 1. The largest age is the victim.

108
00:21:19,169 --> 00:21:23,755
Six. With 16-byte blocks, the last hex digit is the offset.

109
00:21:24,205 --> 00:21:37,624
Seven. N plus 1 blocks in an N-way LRU set miss every time. Video 15 covers misses, writes and the performance of the hierarchy. That is the end of video 14 b.
